@node List and Show Views
@section List and Show Views
@cindex issue list
@cindex issue detail

@deffn Command beads-list-mode
Major mode for a tabulated list of issues.  It renders homogeneous rows
(id, status, priority, type, and title) with clickable headers and a sort
key, and every row is a thing (@pxref{One Movement Scheme}).
@end deffn

@deffn Command beads-show-mode
Major mode for the detail view of one issue.  It renders the issue as a
set of collapsible sections (description, dependencies, comments, history,
and so on).
@end deffn

The two views share the movement and pagination commands below, so the same
keys work in both.

@menu
* View Movement::
* Pagination::
@end menu

@node View Movement
@subsection View Movement
@cindex thing motion

@deffn Command beads-thing-forward
Move to the next thing, wrapping at the end and echoing @samp{Wrapped}.
Bound to @kbd{TAB}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command beads-thing-backward
Move to the previous thing, wrapping at the beginning.  Bound to
@kbd{S-TAB}.
@end deffn

@deffn Command beads-thing-toggle
Run the thing's own toggle (fold, preview) and then the buffer-local
@code{beads-thing-toggle-functions}.  Bound to @kbd{SPC}.
@end deffn

In a tabulated list every row is a thing.  In section buffers the thing is
a run of text carrying the @code{beads-thing} property.  @kbd{n} and
@kbd{p} move to the next and previous line without wrapping.

@node Pagination
@subsection Pagination
@cindex pagination

@deffn Command beads-pager-mode
Minor mode that sizes a page to the window (with a floor of five rows) and
adds navigation for large result sets.
@end deffn

@table @kbd
@kindex ]
@item ]
Move to the next page.
@kindex [
@item [
Move to the previous page.
@kindex G
@item G
Jump to a page.
@kindex g
@item g
Refresh the current view.
@end table

The pagination core is reusable and buffer-agnostic; it is documented
alongside the other extension points.

@cindex server-side pagination
@cindex offset pagination
On a store whose @code{bd} runs against a proxied Dolt server, the list
view pages on the server instead of fetching every matching issue: each
page is one @code{bd list --limit PAGE --offset K} call, filters and the
overall @code{beads-list-default-limit} are preserved, and the mode line
shows a page and item range with @samp{(more)} when another page exists.
The page size is the window-derived pager size, so resizing the window
refetches the current page at the new size; rows that the new size
overlaps from an earlier page are not duplicates.  The total is never
computed, so navigation stops at the first short page.  Jumping past
that page with @kbd{G} shows an empty page (@samp{Page N, no items});
@kbd{[} returns to the last real page.  Embedded and direct stores reject a meaningful
@code{--offset} and keep the client-side pager over a bounded fetch;
@code{bd blocked}, search and the dashboard sections also keep their
existing behavior.

Offset paging is not snapshot-safe.  An issue inserted between two page
fetches can shift rows and repeat one on the next page, so the list
drops any issue id it already showed on an earlier page and an explicit
refresh (@kbd{g}) starts again at page 1.  A silent refresh from the
live journal keeps the current page.  The item range in the mode line
covers the rows actually shown, so dropping a repeated row does not
understate it.

@node Eldoc Previews
@section Eldoc Previews
@cindex eldoc
@cindex issue previews

@deffn Command beads-eldoc-mode
Toggle hover previews of issue references in the current buffer.
@end deffn

@vindex beads-issue-id-regexp
@vindex beads-issue-id-prefixes
@vindex beads-eldoc-auto-enable
Hover over an issue reference anywhere in Emacs --- a comment, a commit
message, an Org file --- and eldoc previews the issue asynchronously.
Loading @code{beads-eldoc} enables @code{beads-eldoc-mode} once by
default, so previews work without any init-file setup; issue ids are
matched with the shared @code{beads-issue-id-regexp} and restricted by
@code{beads-issue-id-prefixes}.

The user option @code{beads-eldoc-auto-enable} controls that automatic
enablement.  Set it to @code{nil} before the library loads to keep the
mode off:

@lisp
(setq beads-eldoc-auto-enable nil)
@end lisp

@noindent
or turn it off later with @kbd{M-x customize-option @key{RET}
beads-eldoc-auto-enable}.  Turning the option off disables the mode only
when this package enabled it; a mode you enabled yourself with
@code{beads-eldoc-mode} is never disabled.  Enabling repeatedly is safe:
the eldoc hook and the cache-invalidation advice are added only once.

Preview data is cached per store, including negative results, so a
reference to an unknown id does not re-query on every keystroke.  Remote
stores are supported; previews use the same asynchronous path as the board
and never block.
